Reprogramming and Immortality: Expression Profiling Reveals Putative Genes Involved in Turritopsis dohrnii’s Life Cycle Reversal\nL36: \nL37: cite13†Yui Matsumoto†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L38: \nL39: ### Yui Matsumoto\nL40: \nL41: ^{1} Department of Marine Biology, Texas A&M University at Galveston, Texas, USA\nL42: \nL43: Find articles by cite13†Yui Matsumoto†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L44: \nL45: ^{1,}^{✉}, cite14†Maria Pia Miglietta†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L46: ### Maria Pia Miglietta\nL47: \nL48: ^{1} Department of Marine Biology, Texas A&M University at Galveston, Texas, USA\nL49: \nL50: Find articles by cite14†Maria Pia Miglietta†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L51: \nL52: ^{1}\nL53: \nL54: Editor: Dennis Lavrov\nL55: \nL56:   * [Button: Author information]\nL57:   * [Button: Article notes]\nL58:   * [Button: Copyright and License information]\nL59: \nL60: ^{1} Department of Marine Biology, Texas A&M University at Galveston, Texas, USA\nL61: \nL62: ^{✉}\nL63: \nL64: Corresponding author: E-mail: yuim@tamu.edu.\nL65: #### Roles\nL66: \nL67: Dennis Lavrov: Associate Editor\nL68: \nL69: Accepted 2021 Jun 10; Collection date 2021 Jul.\nL70: \nL71: © The Author(s) 2021. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of the Society for Molecular Biology and Evolution.\nL72: This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (cite15†http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/†creativecommons.org ), which permits unrestricted reuse, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.\nL73: \nL74: cite16†PMC Copyright notice L75: \nL76: PMCID: PMC8480191  PMID: cite17†34132809†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov L77: ## Abstract\nL78: To gather insight on the genetic network of cell reprogramming and reverse development in a nonmodel cnidarian system, we produced and annotated a transcriptome of the hydrozoan Turritopsis dohrnii, whose medusae respond to damage or senescence by metamorphosing into a juvenile stage (the polyp), briefly passing through an intermediate and uncharacterized stage (the cyst), where cellular transdifferentiation occurs.\nL79: We conducted sequential and pairwise differential gene expression (DGE) analyses of the major life cycle stages involved in the ontogenetic reversal of T. dohrnii. Our DGE analyses of sequential stages of T. dohrnii’s life cycle stages show that novel and characterized genes associated with aging/lifespan, regulation of transposable elements, DNA repair, and damage response, and Ubiquitin-related processes, among others, were enriched in the cyst stage.\nL80: Our pairwise DGE analyses show that, when compared with the colonial polyp, the medusa is enriched with genes involved in membrane transport, the nervous system, components of the mesoglea, and muscle contraction, whereas genes involved in chitin metabolism and the formation of the primary germ layers are suppressed. The colonial polyp and reversed polyp (from cyst) show significant differences in gene expression.\nL81: The reversed polyp is enriched with genes involved in processes such as chromatin remodeling and organization, matrix metalloproteinases, and embryonic development whereas suppressing genes involved in RAC G-protein signaling pathways. In summary, we identify genetic networks potentially involved in the reverse development of T. dohrnii and produce a transcriptome profile of all its life cycle stages, and paving the way for its use as a system for research on cell reprogramming.\nL82: Keywords: reverse development, ontogeny reversal, cell transdifferentiation, RNA-sequencing, differential gene expression\nL83: ## Introduction\nL84: \nL85: Significance\nL86: We investigate the gene expression profiles of life cycle stages of the “Immortal Jellyfish,” Turritopsis dohrnii. We find that the cyst, a unique and intermediate stage between a dying medusa and a new polyp, upregulates genes related to aging/lifespan, transposable elements, DNA repair and damage, and several genes of unknown function.\nL87: We show that polyps, although morphologically identical, show characteristic transcriptome profiling depending on their origin (asexual budding from the colony or reverse development). Overall, we provide insight into genes/transcripts that are responsible for and/or associated with the mechanism of cellular reprogramming that occurs during the reverse development of T. dohrnii.\nL88: The ultimate goal of regenerative research is to replace damaged cells in response to injuries and aging (cite18†Shenoy and Blelloch 2012 ). Transdifferentiation (or cell reprogramming), a process through which a mature somatic cell transforms into a new type of mature somatic cell (cite19†Jopling et al. 2011 ), can achieve this goal.\nL89: In vitro reprogramming of somatic cells has proven to be a powerful tool and has allowed the identification of some of the genetic factors required to change identity (cite20†Dobrovolskaia et al. 2003 ; cite21†Motoyama et al. 2009 ; cite22†Limbert et al. 2011 ).\nL90: However, the mechanisms and molecular drivers by which cells spontaneously (in vivo) leave a differentiated state to become a new lineage are poorly understood (cite23†Merrell and Stanger 2016 ), in large part, because of the difficulties of inducing transdifferentiation in live model systems (cite24†Abad et al. 2013 ; cite25†Alvarado and Yamanaka 2014 ).\nL91: Additionally, because transdifferentiation in available model systems takes place over weeks, modeling its underlying gene regulatory network is problematic (cite26†Kaity et al. 2018 ). Therefore, it is necessary to identify nontraditional species with relevant life history and physiological traits suited for the investigation of genetic mechanism(s) of cellular stability and plasticity in vivo.\nL92: The discovery of reverse development in the cnidarian Turritopsis dohrnii (class Hydrozoa) represents a promising new research system (cite27†Bavestrello et al. 1992 ; cite28†Piraino et al. 1996 ; cite29†Miglietta and Lessios 2009 ). Faced with unfavorable circumstances, the medusa naturally undergoes cellular reprogramming to revert to a younger life cycle stage (the polyp), thus avoiding death indefinitely. Although the majority of hydromedusae become reproductively mature, release gametes, and die, medusae of T.\nL93: dohrnii that are stressed, damaged, or senescent settle on a surface and transform into a cyst stage that, in 24–72 h, metamorphoses back into a single juvenile polyp (cite28†Piraino et al. 1996 ; cite30†Schmich et al. 2007 ; cite31†Matsumoto et al. 2019 ; cite32†Miglietta et al. 2019 ). By asexual reproduction, the polyp can develop into a larger colony (i.e., colonial polyp) that can then release new medusae. Because of its unique life cycle, T. dohrnii has been popularized as the “Immortal Jellyfish.”\nL94: Turritopsis  dohrnii is also heavily understudied, with fewer than a dozen papers published since the discovery of its life cycle (cite25†Alvarado and Yamanaka 2014 ). This is mostly due to the difficulties in collecting T. dohrnii in the field and in handling the species in the laboratory. However, with its unique potential for rejuvenation and the ability to induce cellular reprogramming under controlled laboratory conditions in approximately 24 h (cite28†Piraino et al. 1996 ; cite31†Matsumoto et al. 2019 ), T.\nL95: dohrnii represents a promising albeit unexplored system to investigate molecular mechanisms of cell stability and regeneration that may be relevant to the development of all other animals, including humans.\nL96: Recently, a comparison of three individually assembled de novo transcriptomes of the colonial polyp, cyst, and medusa of T. dohrnii has provided preliminary insight on the differences in the number of transcripts (i.e., raw count) annotated with specific Gene Ontology (GO) terms (cite31†Matsumoto et al. 2019 ). Transcripts associated with telomere organization and maintenance, DNA integration, repair, and damage response were among those that showed high expression in the cyst relative to medusa and polyp.\nL97: Processes associated with cell signaling, division, differentiation, and development showed low expression in the cyst relative to the medusa and polyp. However, the three sequenced libraries (polyp, cyst, and medusa) were not assembled into a single annotated transcriptome because the stages originated from different sampling locations, lacked biological replicates, and were sequenced using different platforms.\nL98: As a result, differential gene expression (DGE) analyses of the life cycle stages were not conducted, and changes in expression levels between the different stages could not be quantified. cite31†Matsumoto et al. (2019) also did not sample reversed polyps (i.e., polyps that originate from the medusa through reverse development).\nL99: Finally, differences in gene activity between the colonial polyp and medusa stage, and polyps developed from different developmental processes, namely asexual budding and reverse development, have yet to be explored.\nL100: We investigate the sequential changes in gene expression that occur during the reverse development of T. dohrnii (from colonial polyp to medusa, to cyst, to reversed polyp), with an emphasis on genes that are upregulated in the cyst, where cellular transdifferentiation occurs.\nL101: We also conduct a pairwise DGE analysis to compare the transcriptional profiles of the benthic colonial polyp and planktonic medusa, and those of the colonial polyp, developed in the wild through asexual budding within a larger colony, and the reversed polyp, developed from the cyst through cellular reprogramming. These analyses reveal differences in gene activity between ecologically and morphologically different stages (benthic and colonial polyp vs.\nL102: planktonic and solitary medusa) and between morphologically similar stages (polyps) generated by two very different developmental pathways, asexual budding and reverse development.\nL103: In summary, this research aims to clarify the genetic pathways involved in the reverse development and cell transdifferentiation of T. dohrnii through sequential and pairwise transcriptomic comparison of life cycle stages involved in both forward and reverse development. These genomic tools will further the potential of T. dohrnii as a system for the study of the mechanisms and molecular drivers by which cells spontaneously leave a differentiated state to become a new lineage.\nL104: ## Results\nL105: ### Transcriptome Assembly and Characterization\nL106: The Metazoa benchmarking universal single-copy orthologs (BUSCO) (cite33†Simão et al. 2015 ) analysis reported 97.9% completeness (95.6% complete, 2.2% partial), indicating that our initial transcriptome assembly is highly complete in terms of gene content (supplementary Appendix A, cite34†Supplementary Material online). The final transcriptome assembly (∼265.685 Mbp) resulted in 204,031 transcripts and 127,645 unigenes with a GC content of 38.29% (supplementary Appendix B, cite34†Supplementary Material online).\nL107: The N50 of the transcriptome is 1,734 bp with a median contig length of 832 bp and an average length of 1,258.07 bp. Approximately 80% of the transcriptome (162,010 out of 204,031 contigs) was annotated with the following annotation pipelines: B2G (NCBI Nonredundant), InterProScan, COG/EggNOG, KEGG, Rfam, and the Hydrozoa EST; and GO terms were found among ∼42% (85,960 out of 204,031) of contigs of the transcriptome (supplementary Appendix C, cite34†Supplementary Material online).\nL108: The majority of the top-BLAST (basic local alignment search tool) hits belonged to six cnidarian species, Hydra vulgaris, Exaiptasia pallida, Stylophora pistillata, Acropora digitifera, Orbicella faveolata, and Nematostella vectensis, ranked 1st to 6th, accounting for ∼52% (59,859 out of 116,110) of total hits [cite35†fig. 1] .\nL109: #### Fig. 1.\nL110: \nL111: cite36†Open in a new tab L112: \nL113: Species distribution of top BLASTx hits in final assembly. Total contigs with BLASTx hit(s): 116,110 transcripts; Blue font: Cnidarian taxa.\nL114: ### DGE Analysis of Life Cycle Stages Involved in Reverse Development\nL115: A DGE analysis of the life cycle stages involved in reverse development (cite37†Nueda et al. 2014 ) was performed in the following sequence: 1) Colonial Polyp, 2) Medusa, 3) Cyst, and 4) Reversed Polyp. This sequence reflects the natural ontogenetic trajectory observed in T. dohrnii during its life cycle reversal. Overall data set quality was verified with a multidimensional scaling plot (supplementary Appendix D, cite34†Supplementary Material online).\nL116: A total of 7,003 differentially expressed genes (DEGs) were identified, where 1,585 DEGs had R^{2} values larger than 0.7 and considered significant for analyses with less than five biological replicates per stage (as recommended by cite37†Nueda et al. [2014 ]) (cite38†fig. 2A ). Nine different gene expression profiles (i.e., clusters) were formed through hierarchical clustering, including 50–239 genes per group (cite38†fig. 2B; expression profiles in supplementary Appendix E, cite34†Supplementary Material online).\nL117: Among the nine clusters, Cluster 5 with 224 genes represented genes that were exclusive and/or associated to the Cyst stage, reporting a statistically significant increase and subsequent decrease in gene activity during the Medusa to Reversed Polyp transition (cite38†fig. 2B [Green] and C).\nL118: Cluster 5 showed a higher number of novel genes (i.e., genes with no form of annotation) (26.8%) compared with the overall transcriptome (20.7%), particularly in the top 50 (44%) and 100 (38%) most significantly DEGs (cite39†table 1A ). The 44% among the top 50 DEGs being novel is a striking comparison to the 20.7% of unannotated sequences in the overall transcriptome. The most significant DEG among the 224 genes was a gene with no annotation (Td_DN103197_c0_g1). It had no evidence of any expression in all T.\nL119: dohrnii samples except for the Cyst replicates (cite40†fig. 3A ). Additionally, there were other unannotated genes that showed a significant peak at the Cyst, such as gene Td_DN92408_c0_g2 (cite40†fig. 3B ).\nL120: #### Fig. 2.\nL121: \nL122: cite41†Open in a new tab L123: \nL124: Results from the DGE analysis of T. dohrnii’s life cycle reversal. (A) Number of significant DEGs. (B) Number of significant DE genes in each profile cluster based on hierarchical clustering. (C) Averaged gene expression profile of Cluster 5 with 224 DEGs, where expression values that are closer to 0 indicate suppressed genes, larger values indicate enriched genes. P, Polyp (from wild colony); M, Medusa; C, Cyst; RP, Reversed Polyp; Green, Genes associated with Cyst.\nL125: #### Table 1.\nL126: \nL127: Annotations of DEGs in Cluster 5\nL128: \nL129: (A) Categories of GO terms and the number of GO terms within category among the top 50, top 100, and all 224 genes.\nL130: \nL131: (B) Gene codes, BLAST protein assignment, and GO function (GO term ID) of representative DEGs for each named category. The expression profiles for the listed DEGs are found in cite40†figure 3 .\nL132: \nL133:  | No. of Sequences\nL134: \nL135: * * *\nL136: --- | ---\nL137: Categories  | Top 50  | Top 100  | All 224 genes\nL138: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL139: Unannotated (novel genes)  | 22 (44%)  | 38 (38%)  | 60 (26.8%)\nL140: Aging and Lifespan  | 2  | 4  | 10\nL141: Transposable elements and DNA integration  | 4  | 7  | 10\nL142: DNA repair and damage response  | 0  | 4  | 13\nL143: Cancer and tumor  | 3  | 3  | 4\nL144: Cell and tissue differentiation  | 2  | 4  | 16\nL145: Development  | 5  | 15  | 60\nL146: Gene Codes  | BLAST Protein Assignment  | GO function (GO Term ID)  | Representative Category\nL147: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL148: Td_DN103197_c0_g1  | N/A  | N/A  | Unannotated\nL149: Td_DN92408_c0_g2  | N/A  | N/A  | Unannotated\nL150: Td_DN99579_c2_g3  | Serine racemase-like gene  | Aging (GO:0007568)  | Aging and Lifespan\nL151: Td_DN111257_c5_g5  | Peptide methionine sulfoxide reductase-like (MsrA)  | Determination of adult lifespan (GO:0008340)  | Aging and Lifespan\nL152: Td_DN111173_c2_g5  | Transposable element Tc3 transposase  | Transposition, DNA-mediated (GO:0006313)  | Transposable elements and DNA integration\nL153: DNA integration (GO:0015074)\nL154: Td_DN108173_c0_g1  | Uncharacterized protein LOC110239820  | DNA integration (GO:0015074)  | Transposable elements and DNA integration\nL155: Td_DN110869_c3_g4  | E3 Ubiquitin-protein ligase Torpors  | Mitotic G2 DNA damage checkpoint (GO:0007095)  | DNA repair and damage response\nL156: Intrinsic apoptotic signaling pathway in response to DNA damage (GO:0097193)\nL157: Td_DN103087_c0_g3  | Tumor necrosis factor receptor superfamilymember 16-like (TPRG1L)  | Response to wounding (GO:0009611)  | Cancer and tumor\nL158: Induction of programmed cell death (GO:0012501)\nL159: Positive regulation of MAPK cascade (GO:0043410)\nL160: Nerve development (GO:0021675)\nL161: Negative regulation of cell cycle (GO:0045786)\nL162: Td_DN97842_c1_g1  | Protein FEV  | Negative regulation of cell differentiation (GO:0045596)  | Cell and tissue differentiation\nL163: Cell fate determination (GO:0001709)\nL164: Stress-activated MAPK cascade (GO:0051403)\nL165: Td_DN105664_c0_g1  | Methionine aminopeptidase 2  | Wnt signaling pathway (GO:0016055)  | Cell and tissue differentiation\nL166: Stem cell differentiation (GO:0048863)\nL167: * * *\nL168: \nL169: cite42†Open in a new tab L170: \nL171: Note.—MAPK, mitogen-activated protein kinase.\nL172: #### Fig. 3.\nL173: \nL174: cite43†Open in a new tab L175: Expression profile of DEGs: (A) Expression profile of an unannotated gene Td_DN103197_c0_g1 from cluster 5; (B) Expression profile of an unannotated gene Td_DN92408_c0_g2 from cluster 5; (C) Serine racemase-like (Td_DN99579_c2_g3); (D) Peptide methionine sulfoxide reductase-like (Td_DN111257_c5_g5); (E) Transposable element Tc3 Transposase (Td_DN111173_c2_g5); (F) Uncharacterized protein LOC110239820 (Td_DN108173_c0_g1); (G) E3 Ubiquitin-protein ligase Torpors (Td_DN110869_c3_g4); (H) Tumor necrosis factor receptor superfamily member 16-like (Td_DN103087_c0_g3); (I) Protein FEV (Td_DN97842_c1_g1); and (J) Methionine aminopeptidase 2 (Td_DN105664_c0_g1).\nL176: #### Representative DEGs for Each Annotation Category\nL177: Besides several unannotated genes (see cite40†fig. 3A and B ), genes associated with aging and lifespan, transposable elements, DNA repair and damage response, cancer/tumors, and cell differentiation and development were found in Cluster 5 (cite39†table 1 and cite40†fig. 3C–H ). Representative DEGs for each category, with their gene code, BLAST protein assignment, GO function, and term IDs, are reported in detail in cite39†table 1B .\nL178: Below we summarize our findings and highlight annotation categories and their representative DEGs found in Cluster 5 that show potential to be involved in the cellular reprogramming and reverse development process in T. dohrnii.\nL179: We report genes involved in lifespan and aging (i.e., “Serine-racemase-like” and “Peptide methionine sulfoxide reductase-like [MsrA])” and in transposition and DNA integration (cite39†table 1 and cite38†fig. 2C–F ). Among them is “Transposable element Tc3 transposase,” largely explored in Caenorhabditis  elegans as one of the most active transposons (cite44†Bessereau 2006 ).\nL180: Genes associated to DNA repair and response to DNA damage, such as Ubiquitin-related genes, proteins that target cellular destruction by proteasomes in response to DNA damage, were also found in Cluster 5 (cite39†table 1 and cite38†fig. 2G ). Several cancer and tumor-related genes were also reported.\nL181: Among them were “Breast cancer type 1 susceptibility protein isoform X1 BRCA1),” “Tumor protein p63-regulated gene 1-like protein (TPRG1L),” and “Tumor necrosis factor receptor superfamily member 16-like (Tnfrsf16)” (supplementary Appendix F, cite34†Supplementary Material online and cite38†fig. 2H ).\nL182: Finally, we report numerous genes associated with cell/tissue differentiation and development, such as “Protein PEV” and “Methionine aminopeptidase 2” with functions related to cell fate determination and embryonic development, respectively (cite39†table 1 and cite40†fig. 3I and J ).\nL183: A functional gene enrichment analysis of Cluster 5 reported that terms associated with larval development, the response to DNA damage, and protein monoubiquitination were among the most enriched (supplementary Appendix E, cite34†Supplementary Material online).\nL184: On the other hand, processes associated with cytoskeleton and chromosome organization, both specific child-GO terms of broader mitotic cell division processes, were among the most suppressed in Cluster 5 (supplementary Appendix E, cite34†Supplementary Material online).\nL185: ### Pairwise DGE Analyses\nL186: \nL187: Pairwise DGE analyses were performed to identify differences in transcriptional profile between 1) Colonial Polyp and Reversed Polyp and 2) Polyp and Medusa. Due to the large number of total DEGs in both comparisons, only the top 50 were further analyzed (cite45†fig. 4 ). DEGs and functional categories that may reflect important physiological differences between the life cycle stages are reported below and summarized in cite46†table 2 .\nL188: #### Fig. 4.\nL189: \nL190: cite47†Open in a new tab L191: \nL192: (A) Number of overexpressed and underexpressed genes in the Reversed Polyp versus Polyp, and Medusa versus Polyp pairwise DGE analyses. (B, C) Visualization of the top 50 most DEGs (red: overexpressed, green: underexpressed) when Polyp was compared with the Reversed Polyp and Medusa was compared with the Polyp. Heatmap was created by the Heatmapper software (cite48†Babicki et al. 2016 ).\nL193: #### Table 2.\nL194: \nL195: Summary of Pairwise DGE Analyses (Reversed Polyp vs Colonial Polyp; Medusa vs Polyp)\nL196: Analysis  | Representative Genes  | Funtional Category\nL197: --- | --- | ---\nL198: Reversed Polyp vs Colonial Polyp  | Bromodomain adjacent to zinc finger domain protein 1A (BAZ1A) (cite49†Oppikofer et al. 2017 )  | Chromatin remodeling\nL199: Sushi, von Willebrand factor type A, EGF, and pentraxin domain-containing protein 1 (SVEP1) (cite50†Shur et al. 2006 )  | Chromatin remodeling\nL200: Matrix Metalloproteinases (MMPs)-II; MMP-14 isoform X1  | MMPs\nL201: Thyrotroph embryonic factor-like (TEF) (cite51†Gavriouchkina et al. 2010 )  | Embryonic development\nL202: Medusa vs Polyp  | ATP-binding cassette sub-family A member 3-like (ABCA3)  | Cellular and transmembrane transport\nL203: Collagen alpha-1, Collagen alpha-6 chain  | Collagen\nL204: Clathrin heavy chain (cite52†Vassilopoulos et al. 2014 )  | Muscle development/assembly and contraction\nL205: Actin (cite52†Vassilopoulos et al. 2014 )\nL206: Myosin (cite53†Rayment et al. 1993 )\nL207: Calmodulin (cite54†Chin 2005 )\nL208: Dystroglycan (cite55†Adams and Brancaccio 2015 )\nL209: Elongation Factor 1A (cite56†Murray et al. 1996 )\nL210: Dystroglycan (cite57†Yatsenko and Shcherbata 2014 )  | Nervous system development\nL211: Clathrin heavy chain (cite58†Sato et al. 2009 )\nL212: cite59†Open in a new tab L213: #### Colonial Polyp Versus Reversed Polyp\nL214: While sharing 16,614 transcripts, the Reversed Polyp had a larger number of unique transcripts when compared to both the colonial polyp and to the rest of the stages (respectively, 11,603 and 9,729). The majority of the 50 most significant DEGs were enriched in the Reversed Polyp and suppressed in the Colonial Polyp (cite45†fig. 4A and B and cite34†supplementary Appendix H, cite34†Supplementary Material online).\nL215: The most enriched gene in the Reversed Polyp was “Bromodomain adjacent to zinc finger domain protein 1A (BAZ1A),” followed by “Sushi, von Willebrand factor type A, EGF, and pentraxin domain-containing protein 1 (SVEP1)” (cite34†supplementary Appendix H, cite34†Supplementary Material online), both involved in chromatin remodeling (cite60†Wang et al. 2004 ; cite61†Zaghlool et al. 2016 ). Matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs), such as MMP-II and MMP-14 isoform X1’, were also upregulated in the Reversed Polyp.\nL216: Furthermore, “Thyrotroph embryonic factor-like(TEF)”, a gene active during mammalian embryogenesis (cite62†Drolet et al. 1991 ), with GO terms associated to embryonic development, was overexpressed in the Reversed Polyp (cite34†supplementary Appendix H, cite34†Supplementary Material online).\nL217: An enrichment analysis using all significant DEGs showed the Reversed Polyp enriched in a variety of GO categories, such as reproduction, development/growth, symbiotic processes, actin filaments/microtubule, and response to stimuli (cite63†fig. 5A ). On the other hand, the Colonial Polyp was enriched in genes associated with the regulation of RAC protein signaling transduction and G-protein receptor signaling pathway (cite63†fig.\nL218: 5A ), both overlapping processes that use Rho GTP-binding proteins to initiate signaling cascades (cite64†Ridley 2006 ).\nL219: ##### Fig. 5.\nL220: \nL221: cite65†Open in a new tab L222: \nL223: Functional gene enrichment analyses. (A) Reversed Polyp versus Polyp and (B) Medusa versus Polyp analysis, where significantly enriched and suppressed categories were reduced to the most specific terms (sorted by most different between Reversed Polyp/Medusa and Polyp).\nL224: ##### Medusa Versus Polyp\nL225: In the Medusa versus Polyp comparison, the majority of the most significant 50 DEGs were overexpressed in the Medusa, with only three annotated genes overexpressed in the Polyp (i.e., Ectin-like, Galaxin-like, Prefoldin subunit 5 [cite34†supplementary Appendix H, cite34†Supplementary Material online]). Notably, in the 50 most significant DEGs, 17 (34%) transcripts were novel (cite34†supplementary Appendix H, cite34†Supplementary Material online).\nL226: This is substantially higher than the Polyp versus Reversed Polyp comparison that had 10 (20%) novel transcripts (cite34†supplementary Appendix H, cite34†Supplementary Material online). The most enriched gene in the Medusa was “ATP-binding cassette sub-family A member 3-like (ABCA3)” (cite34†supplementary Appendix H, cite34†Supplementary Material online).\nL227: In addition to ABCA3, five other genes with GO terms involving cellular and transmembrane transport were overexpressed (cite34†supplementary Appendix H, cite34†Supplementary Material online).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe \"Immortal\" Jellyfish That Resets When Damaged | AMNH (https://www.amnh.org/explore/news-blogs/on-exhibit-posts/the-immortal-jellyfish)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.amnh.org/explore/news-blogs/on-exhibit-posts/the-immortal-jellyfish\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.amnh.org/explore/news-blogs/immortal-jellyfish; Total lines: 252\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReversing the Life Cycle: Medusae Transforming into Polyps and Cell Transdifferentiation in Turritopsis nutricula (Cnidaria, Hydrozoa) | The Biological Bulletin: Vol 190, No 3 (https://doi.org/10.2307/1543022)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.2307/1543022\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.2307/1543022; Total lines: 248\n"
